WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.759 --> 00:00:07.979
We all love the company of our favorite people!

00:00:07.979 --> 00:00:12.830
Similarly, Hydrocarbon chains are also fond
of other molecules.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:16.180
They get bonded
so strongly that the properties of the compound

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:17.400
change depending on the

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.660
molecules attached.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:22.800
And what are these group of molecules referred
to as?

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.170
They
are called as the Functional Groups, that’s

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:26.170
right!

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:28.560
Let us get to know about them.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.090
Beginning with the definition first!

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:32.570
How do we define them?

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:36.559
In simple words,
these are the group of atoms that give characteristic

00:00:36.559 --> 00:00:40.579
properties to the compounds
in which they are present.

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:41.579
Yes!

00:00:41.579 --> 00:00:44.079
Their presence is quite influential!

00:00:44.079 --> 00:00:47.280
If they’re a part
of a compound, the entire compound takes its

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.760
properties!

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.489
Have we come across any of the functional
groups ever?

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:52.489
Yes!

00:00:52.489 --> 00:00:57.230
We’ve heard many
names like Alcohols, Phenols, Ethers and so

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:58.230
on!

00:00:58.230 --> 00:00:59.230
Animators: Show all the below ones with structures:

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:00.889
Look at the structures carefully!

00:01:00.889 --> 00:01:01.889
What do you notice?

00:01:01.889 --> 00:01:06.790
It helps us understand that they have atoms
which can form at least one bond

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:08.620
with any other atom.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:11.720
Yes at least one atom in the complete group
is ready for

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:12.810
bond formation.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:13.991
That is the reason why they can easily bond
with any other atom and become a

00:01:13.991 --> 00:01:14.991
part of any compound easily.

00:01:14.991 --> 00:01:16.170
Let’s understand this in a simple way.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:20.250
Say we focus on this structure of Alcohol.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:25.040
This functional group has one oxygen
atom and one hydrogen atom.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.670
That means a hydroxyl unit is what makes up
the

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.860
alcoholic group.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.550
Can you notice that we have drawn a line before
oxygen here?

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:34.950
What does this
indicate?

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:40.260
Well, it indicates that the group is available
for bond formation!

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.410
So
there can be any other atom bonded to the

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:45.790
oxygen atom of this hydroxyl unit!

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:50.100
So if we take any carbon chain, say something
like this, then we can attach the

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:54.280
alcoholic functional group at the carbon atom
which is available for bond

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.820
formation.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:58.690
And the new compound gets formed in this way!

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:03.520
Similarly, talking about the functional group
CARBOXYLIC ACID, its structure is

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.270
somewhat like this!

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:11.530
Represented as dash followed by COOH, the
group contains one carbon atom!

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:16.590
Out of the 4 bonds that it can form, 3 are
already formed.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.950
As we can see here,
one oxygen atom is attached to the carbon

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:25.540
atom with a DOUBLE BOND while
another single bond is with a hydroxyl group.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:30.450
This is how we get COOH as our
carboxyl group.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:35.030
Now from from where will this group be attached
to the hydrocarbon chain?

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:36.030
Yes!

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.469
From the carbon atom!

00:02:37.469 --> 00:02:38.920
And why is that?

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:42.129
We know that the valency of carbon is
FOUR.

00:02:42.129 --> 00:02:46.430
Three bonds in the functional group have got
formed leaving behind just

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:47.709
one.

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:51.629
That is why the line is drawn before Carbon
atom which indicates that the

00:02:51.629 --> 00:02:53.620
group can be attached from here!

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:57.480
Where do you think this group will be attached?

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.180
It can be attached to an Alkyl
group!

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:03.450
Wait…what do we mean by an alkyl group?

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:08.340
We’ve seen what Alkanes, alkenes and alkynes
are!

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.290
Single bonds: Alkanes!

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.519
At
least one doube bond: Alkenes.

00:03:12.519 --> 00:03:16.139
And at least one triple bond: Alkynes!

00:03:16.139 --> 00:03:18.459
So what is
an alkyl group then?

00:03:18.459 --> 00:03:21.799
Let’s take an example of ethane to understand
this!

00:03:21.799 --> 00:03:27.340
We can see that each carbon atom here has
its valencies completely fulfiled.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:28.900
Four
and four!

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:32.809
However, if we remove even one hydrogen atom,
then the group becomes

00:03:32.809 --> 00:03:36.170
capable of forming bonds with other compounds.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:40.739
Like in this case it can bond
with a functional group from this point!

00:03:40.739 --> 00:03:44.290
This thing that you see here is an ALKYL
group.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:48.659
It is just derived from an alkane by removing
on of its hydrogen atoms!

00:03:48.659 --> 00:03:50.439
That’s all.

00:03:50.439 --> 00:03:52.849
Here it has been derived from ethane!

00:03:52.849 --> 00:03:55.959
Hence it’s called an ETHYL group!

00:03:55.959 --> 00:04:00.510
Similarly, if we remove one hydrogen atom
from Methane, it will be called a

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:01.969
METHYL group!

00:04:01.969 --> 00:04:05.629
Alkyl groups are usually represented with
the letter “R” written in

00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:07.290
upper case.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:10.909
This was a general overview of what alkyl
groups are!

00:04:10.909 --> 00:04:13.930
But the main topic of this video was functional
groups.

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:17.850
They’re a group of atoms
that give characteristic properties to the

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:20.160
compounds in which they are present.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:22.140
The entire compound takes its properties!

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:26.630
Let us have a look at a few more
interesting examples of functional groups

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:29.750
in the next video!

